## Create a session

```ts
import { SandboxAgent } from "sandbox-agent";

const sdk = await SandboxAgent.connect({
  baseUrl: "http://127.0.0.1:2468",
});

const session = await sdk.createSession({
  agent: "codex",
  cwd: "/",
});

console.log(session.id, session.agentSessionId);
```

With model and mode, set at creation time:

```ts
const session = await sdk.createSession({
  agent: "codex",
  model: "gpt-5.3-codex",
  mode: "auto",
  thoughtLevel: "high",
});
```

## Send a prompt

```ts
const response = await session.prompt([
  { type: "text", text: "Summarize the repository structure." },
]);

console.log(response.stopReason);
```

## Prompting while a turn is running

The doc doesn't say whether a prompt sent during an active turn is queued or interrupts the current turn.

## Cancel

The doc doesn't describe a cancel method. The only lifecycle call shown is destroying the session:

```ts
await sdk.destroySession(session.id);
```

## Events

Live subscription:

```ts
const unsubscribe = session.onEvent((event) => {
  console.log(event.eventIndex, event.sender, event.payload);
});

await session.prompt([
  { type: "text", text: "Explain the main entrypoints." },
]);

unsubscribe();
```

Persisted history:

```ts
const page = await sdk.getEvents({
  sessionId: session.id,
  limit: 50,
});

for (const event of page.items) {
  console.log(event.id, event.createdAt, event.sender);
}
```

Each event's `payload` is a session update identified by `sessionUpdate`. The documented types are `agent_message_chunk`, `agent_thought_chunk`, `user_message_chunk`, `tool_call`, `tool_call_update`, `plan`, `usage_update`, and `session_info_update`.

## Permission requests and replies

```ts
const session = await sdk.createSession({
  agent: "claude",
  mode: "default",
});

session.onPermissionRequest((request) => {
  console.log(request.toolCall.title, request.availableReplies);
  void session.respondPermission(request.id, "once");
});

await session.prompt([
  { type: "text", text: "Create ./permission-example.txt with the text hello." },
]);
```

Replies are `once`, `always`, or `reject`. To auto-approve everything:

```ts
session.onPermissionRequest((request) => {
  void session.respondPermission(request.id, "always");
});
```

The doc recommends keeping the mode as `default` and handling decisions in `onPermissionRequest`, rather than using modes like `bypassPermissions` or `acceptEdits`.

## Session restore

Sessions can be restored after runtime or session loss when persistence is enabled. The doc points to Session Restoration for details.

```ts
const sessions = await sdk.listSessions({ limit: 20 });

for (const item of sessions.items) {
  console.log(item.id, item.agent, item.createdAt);
}

if (sessions.items.length > 0) {
  const loaded = await sdk.resumeSession(sessions.items[0]!.id);
  await loaded.prompt([{ type: "text", text: "Continue." }]);
}
```

## ACP

The page never explains ACP. It appears only in the sidebar's HTTP API list as endpoints (`get_v1_acp_servers`, `get_v1_acp`, `post_v1_acp`, `delete_v1_acp`). The event payload shape, with `sessionUpdate` and `toolCallId` fields, may follow ACP conventions, but the page doesn't say so.

